      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:47 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners (Amy M. Saharia)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SAHARIA: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: TPS recipients satisfy the admission requirement for adjustment to lawful permanent resident status. That is because Section 1254a(f)(4) considers TPS recipients to be in lawful status as a non-immigrant for purposes of adjusting status, and having been admitted is inherent in non-immigrant status."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, as I understand your argument, it's that people in non-immigrant status go through a process to get there that includes admission and inspection. And there's another way to get to non-immigrant status, and that is by being a TPS recipient. And your argument seems to me to be that, well, if you're in non-immigrant status as a TPS recipient, you must have been admitted and inspected or treated as such because that's the other way to get to non-immigrant status. And I wonder why they're just not two different routes, and if you come in one route, the TPS route, that doesn't mean that you've checked every box to get in through the other route. So it seems to me that I -- I can't follow the logic of your main submission."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, what's on -- your -- your argument is that the status -- non-immigrant status necessarily entails inspection. What is -- do you have a case for that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: Good morning. So I have a question about 1254a(h)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice THOMAS: Thank you, Chief Justice -- Mr. Chief Justice. Counsel, as a matter of curiosity, if the Petitioners here were allowed to leave the country for a brief visit abroad and return, would they then be eligible for adjustment of status?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, I'd like to return to Justice Thomas's question about people who have traveled abroad, been given permission, TPS people who have been given permission to travel abroad."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Huston (Government)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUSTON: Justice Sotomayor, the first thing to note about this argument, of course, is that Petitioners have expressly waived it in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Huston (Government)",
      "case_id": "2020-20-315",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c25b7dc70335c165d03f6c6d6751edd7d9ad47cc51f79794ae856cbfcc8e2e4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c25b7dc7033-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HUSTON: No, Your Honor. The agency's position has been that the statute is unambiguous at Chevron step 1, but, in the alternative, if it's ambiguous, it has --it would have reached the same result."
    },
